{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>When you gather in the produce of the land</b> — that this seventh month should occur at the time of gathering. From here they were commanded to intercalate the years, for without intercalation it sometimes falls in the middle of the kayitz (summer) or the winter — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 23:39). And the language of Torat Kohanim (chapter 15, halakhah 7): intercalate the years so that the festival falls at the gathering of the fruits. One might think at the gathering of all the fruits — Scripture says, \"from your threshing floor and from your winepress\" (Deuteronomy 16:13): \"from your threshing floor\" — not all your threshing floor; \"and from your winepress\" — not all your winepress. If it says \"when you gather from your threshing floor,\" one might think only a portion — Scripture says, \"when you gather in the produce of the land.\" How so? Strive to intercalate the year so that the festival falls when the gathering of all the fruits is at its peak. And they already derived the intercalation from the month of the Aviv (Rosh HaShanah 21a), but there it teaches about the Aviv and here it teaches about the fruits of the tree, for thus the Sages taught (Sanhedrin 11a): the year is intercalated for three things — for the Aviv, for the fruits of the tree, and for the tekufah (equinox/season). Now, the intercalation is self-evidently learned from the fact that this festival is at the time of gathering, but the point of the verse's formulation is to say: these are the appointed times of YHWH which you shall proclaim, all of them, as sacred convocations, to offer upon all of them a fire-offering to HaShem — yet on the fifteenth day of this seventh month, when you gather in the produce of the land, you shall celebrate the festival of YHWH for seven days. And it adds here, \"on the first day a shabbaton and on the eighth day a shabbaton\" (Leviticus 23:39) — that you shall rest and be still on them, as I have explained (Ramban on Leviticus 23:24). And it further says that you shall add rejoicing before HaShem with lulav and etrog for seven days, for it is a time of joy — HaShem blessed you in all your produce and in all the work of your hands, and you shall be only joyful before Him. And the reason for \"you shall celebrate the festival of YHWH\" is that you shall celebrate before Him to give thanks to His name that the gathering of your labors has come. And it returned and said further (Leviticus 23:41), \"and you shall celebrate it as a festival to HaShem,\" to attach to it \"you shall dwell in sukkot\" (Leviticus 23:42). And its reason: you shall celebrate it as a festival for seven days in the year when you dwell in sukkot, and every native in Israel shall dwell in sukkot — to say that they shall make a festival of seven days with lulav and rejoicing and in the sukkah. And its midrash: \"and you shall celebrate it as a festival to YHWH\" — what does this teach? Since it is stated, \"seven days you shall celebrate to HaShem your Elohim\" (Deuteronomy 16:15), one might think he brings the festive offering all seven days — Scripture says, \"it\" (otó), etc., in Torat Kohanim (chapter 17, halakhah 1)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The word קַיִץ appears in rabbinic seasonal terminology; I have left it transliterated as 'kayitz' with the parenthetical gloss '(summer)' following Rashi's own usage at this verse."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:58:56.186343163Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}